text:
How much can we teach AI to understand the world just by scaling up head-cam videos? This new 30,000-hour study shows body movement is nearly maxed out—agent fidelity hits 0.81 SCS with just a few thousand hours (or 1,000 h + skeleton conditioning). Piling on 100× more data or bigger models barely helps.

But object dynamics? Still stuck. Even after 30,000 h, object fidelity tops out at 0.56 SCS—30% below the agent ceiling. The only way up: a new object-centric noise scheduling trick, which nudges the model to actually care about hand-object contact, bumps object SCS by 3.7%, and shifts the limit to 0.58.

Key takeaway: collecting more ego-centric data isn’t enough. To build world models that actually get how objects move, we need objectives that focus on the action, not just the pixels.

Get the full analysis here: https://t.co/LeTvDPH2Go

